Node_n03_section_summarizer. We need to produce JSON with "summarized_sections" mapping section titles to short summary up to 10 sentences. Only one section title: "METHODS". So output:

{
  "summarized_sections": {
    "METHODS": "..."
  }
}

We must summarize the METHODS section. Provide up to 10 sentences, plain text. Should capture key background knowledge, study design, instrumentation, cohort characteristics, main findings relevant to glycan biomarker curation. But this is methods; no findings. So summary of methods.

We need to capture: sample sources (50 subjects from 3 studies: 5 healthy controls, 13 MetS, 7 additional healthy controls from methods development, 13 HD+ infection, 13 HD- infection-free). Inclusion criteria. Study registration. Sample processing: blood drawn, centrifugation, shipping, storage. HDL isolation by ultracentrifugation with KBr densities. Measurements: HDL cholesterol, ApoA-I, endotoxin, LBP, etc. Protein standards: ApoC-III, A1AT, A2HSG, SAA. Trypsin digestion, LC-MS/MS (QE-Orbitrap) and LC-QqQ for quantitation. Glycopeptide identification with Byonic, quantification with MRM. Normalization to cholesterol or protein. Glycan composition notation. PBMC isolation, monocyte culture with LPS and HDL, IL-6 measurement via Bio-Plex. HDL size measurement by PCS/QELS. Statistical analysis: R, JMP, Shapiro-Wilk, ANOVA, non-parametric tests, PLS-DA, VIP >1. Glycopeptide categories.

We need up to 10 sentences. Let's craft 9 sentences.

Let's produce JSON.

Node_n06_rel_0. We need to evaluate the relation: glycan_name: "α2-3 sialylation" (Greek alpha). It's a glycan modification. The evidence sentences: "We previously identified PCa-associated aberrant glycosylation on PSA, that is, α2,3-linked sialylation as an additional terminal N-glycan on free PSA(S2,3PSA)." So evidence shows association between α2-3 sialylation on PSA and prostate cancer. Direction: "associated" is fine. The evidence supports association. No direction like higher/lower. So keep.

Check glycan string: "α2-3 sialylation" is fine. No non-glycan components. The metadata "additional terminal N-glycan on free PSA" is not part of glycan name. So no fix needed.

Specimen: serum. That matches evidence: detection in serum. Species: human. Protein: PSA is a protein, but protein_name is null. Should we add protein_name? The evidence refers to PSA. The relation currently has protein_name null. The schema: protein_name field is optional. But the instruction: if specimen/species/protein fields are present and clearly inconsistent with evidence, fix them; otherwise preserve. Here protein_name is null, but evidence mentions PSA. Should we add protein_name? The relation didn't include protein_name. The instruction says: "If specimen/species/protein fields are present and clearly inconsistent with evidence, fix them; otherwise preserve the input." The protein field is present but null. Null is not inconsistent. So we can keep as is. But maybe we should add protein_name "PSA" because it's relevant. But the instruction says we can keep. But we might consider adding protein_name to reflect the glycan is on PSA. However, the relation's glycan_name is just the modification; the protein is separate. The evidence indicates the glycan is on PSA. The relation currently has protein_name null. Should we set protein_name to PSA? The instruction: "If specimen/species/protein fields are present and clearly inconsistent with evidence, fix them; otherwise preserve the input." So we don't need to fix. But we might consider adding protein_name because it's relevant. But the instruction says we can preserve. But we might want to add protein_name to be more accurate. But the instruction says we can preserve. But we might not want to add new fields not present. The output format: we can include protein_name only if we change it. Since we are not changing, we omit. So action: keep.

We need to output JSON with relations list containing one object. Since action keep, we only include original_index, action, reason. original_index is relation_index? The input uses "relation_index": 0. So original_index: 0.

Reason: evidence supports association between α2-3 sialylation on PSA and prostate cancer; direction "associated" is appropriate.

Thus output:

{
  "relations": [
    {
      "original_index": 0,
      "action": "keep",
      "reason": "Evidence sentence explicitly links α2-3 sialylation on PSA to prostate cancer, supporting an association; direction 'associated' is appropriate."
    }
  ]
}

Check format: no extra fields. Good.

Node_n06_rel_2. We need to evaluate the relation. Glycan: STn (sialyl-Tn antigen). Disease: bladder cancer invasion front. Direction: increased. Evidence sentences: "The STn antigen was mainly present at the cell surface and, in less extent, in the cytoplasm." "It presented a focal expression throughout the tissues that did not exceed 30% of the tumour area, being frequently observed at tumour invasion fronts." The title/abstract mention STn overexpression in invasive bladder tumors, and association with invasion. The evidence sentences mention STn present at cell surface, focal expression, frequently observed at tumor invasion fronts. They don't explicitly say increased compared to normal tissue or other areas. But the abstract says "Invasive bladder tumours express the cell-surface Sialyl-Tn (STn) antigen...". The evidence sentences are from the article, but they don't mention comparison to normal or other disease states. They just say STn present at cell surface, focal expression, frequently observed at invasion fronts. That suggests association with invasion front, but not necessarily increased relative to other areas. However, the abstract says overexpression in all cell lines under hypoxia. But the evidence sentences given are not about overexpression. They are about presence. So direction "increased" may not be supported by evidence sentences. We need to check if evidence sentences contain explicit link between glycan and disease/disorder. They mention "tumour invasion fronts" but not "bladder cancer". The disease context is "bladder cancer invasion front". The evidence sentences mention "tumour area" and "tumour invasion fronts". That is consistent. But direction: "increased" is not supported. We could downgrade to "associated". The evidence indicates presence at invasion front, but not necessarily increased relative to other tissues. So action: "fix" with direction changed to "associated". Also check glycan string: "STn" is fine. No non-glycan. So we keep glycan. No need to change glycan. So we output action "fix" with reason. We need to include glycan_normalized? Only if correcting glycan_name. We are not changing glycan_name. So we don't include glycan_normalized. We need to include "split_biomarkers"? No. So output with action "fix" and reason. Also we might need to adjust direction? The schema expects direction field in relation. But we are not outputting the updated relation; we just output the action and reason. The instructions: For fix, include glycan_normalized only when correcting glycan_name. We are not correcting glycan_name. So we don't include glycan_normalized. We also don't include split_biomarkers. So output:

{
  "relations": [
    {
      "original_index": 2,
      "action": "fix",
      "reason": "Evidence shows STn present at invasion fronts but does not explicitly state increased levels; downgrade direction to associated."
    }
  ]
}

Check if we need to include any other fields? The schema says: If action is keep or reject: omit glycan_normalized and omit split_biomarkers. So we can just include original_index, action, reason. Good.

Double-check: The evidence sentences mention "focal expression throughout the tissues that did not exceed 30% of the tumour area, being frequently observed at tumour invasion fronts." That indicates presence but not necessarily increased relative to normal. So direction "increased" is unsupported. So fix to "associated". Good.

